%I A398494 #30 Aug 30 2026 23:28:18
%S A398494 41,233,251,397,431,509,641,827,1019
%N A398494 Primes p such that 2^p-1 has a divisor d with 1<d<2^p-1 and d == 1-2*p (mod 2*p^2).
%C A398494 This sequence records the case k == -1 (mod p) in the standard representation d = 2*p*k+1 of Mersenne divisors.
%C A398494 a(10) >= 1213.
%C A398494 6977 is a term.
%C A398494 For n <= 9, a qualifying prime divisor exists exactly for n=1, 3, 8.
%C A398494 For m = 4, the only terms p <= 10^9 are 41 and 6977; in this case d is necessarily prime.
%H A398494 S. S. Wagstaff, Jr., <a href="https://homes.cerias.purdue.edu/~ssw/cun/pmain126.txt">Main Tables from the Cunningham Project</a>.
%F A398494 A qualifying divisor is equivalently of the form d = 2*p*(m*p-1)+1 = 2*m*p^2-2*p+1 for some integer m >= 1. For m = 4, d = 8*p^2-2*p+1.
%e A398494 For a(2)=233, d=1399*135607*622577=118111693135361 is a proper divisor of 2^233-1 and d==1-2*233 (mod 2*233^2), so 233 is a term.
%o A398494 (PARI) isok(p) = { if(!isprime(p), return(0)); my(M=2^p-1); fordiv(M, d, if(1<d && d<M && (d+2*p-1)%(2*p^2)==0, return(1))); return(0); }
%Y A398494 Cf. A135975.
%K A398494 nonn,more,new
%O A398494 1,1
%A A398494 _A. Lamek_, Jul 31 2026
